1.0	SOFTWARE PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Product Name:		COP8-DEV-IBMA

Software Part Number:	925421632-001

Description:		COP8 Assembler/Linker/Librarian & Utilities, Version 4.5

System:			MS-DOS (R) 3.1 or later (3.5 inch PC-DOS (R) format)

This release package contains software to support development of programs for
National Semiconductor's COP8 series of microcontrollers.

The software executes on an IBM PC/AT or compatible running MS-DOS or PC-DOS.
The package consists of the following programs:
 ______________________________________________________________________
|Name:      |Version:   |Date:        |Description:                    |
|___________|___________|_____________|________________________________|
|  ASMCOP   |    4.5    |  24 Feb 95  | Cross-assembler for COP8       |
|___________|___________|_____________|________________________________|
|  LNCOP    |    4.5    |  24 Feb 95  | Cross-linker for COP8          |
|___________|___________|_____________|________________________________|
|  LIBCOP   |    4.5    |  24 Feb 95  | Cross-librarian for COP8       |
|___________|___________|_____________|________________________________|
|  PROMCOP  |    4.0    | 30 Sept 92  | Utility program to convert COFF|
|           |           |             | files for burning proms        |
|___________|___________|_____________|________________________________|
|  DUMPCOFF |    4.0    | 30 Sept 92  | Utility program to display COFF|
|           |           |             | information                    |
|___________|___________|_____________|________________________________|
|  HEXDIFF  |    4.0    | 30 Sept 92  | Utility program to compare     |
|           |           |             | Hex/LM files                   |
|___________|___________|_____________|________________________________|
|  HEXLM    |  Rev. D   |  27 Jul 87  | Utility program to convert HEX |
|           |           |             | files to LM format             |
|___________|___________|_____________|________________________________|
|  LMHEX    |  Rev. D   |  27 Jul 87  | Utility program to convert LM  |
|           |           |             | files to HEX format            |
|___________|___________|_____________|________________________________|


2.0	OPERATING ENVIRONMENT

This package has been developed and tested on an IBM PC/AT running PC-DOS 5.0.
It runs under the MS-DOS or PC-DOS operating system, Version 3.1 or later, on
an IBM PC/AT or compatible.  The system requirements are:

o  At least 256 Kbytes of memory.

o  A 3.5 inch floppy drive capable of reading double sided, high density format
   (1.44 MB) diskettes.

o  An asynchronous, RS232-compatible serial port for communication.


3.0	ENHANCEMENTS 

Support for the COP888GD chip has been added to Version 4.5.  Default ranges
for this chip are given in the following table:

 __________________________________________________________________________
|  CHIP      | ROM     | RAM       | BASE  | REG      | SEG      | SEGB    |
|____________|_________|___________|_______|__________|__________|_________|
|  COP888GD  | 0:3FFF  | 0:6F,REG  | 0:F   | F0:FB    | 100:17F  | 100:10F |    
|            |         |           |       |          | 200:27F  | 200:20F |    
|            |         |           |       |          | 300:37F  | 300:30F |    
|____________|_________|___________|_______|__________|__________|_________|

For more details, see the COP8 Assembler/Linker/Librarian User's Manual
and the appropriate data sheet.


4.0	COMPATIBILITY ISSUES

4.1	METALINK ICEMASTER(TM)-COP8

This software is compatible with the COP8 MetaLink debugger, iceMASTER-COP8 3.2,
Rev. 11 and above.


4.2	DBCOP

This software is not compatible with the debugger DBCOP.


5.0	INSTALLATION PROCEDURE

Place the COP8-DEV-IBMA disk into a floppy drive.  Enter:

   	<drive>INSTALL		(Where <drive> is either A: or B:)

INSTALL will prompt for the drive letter of the source drive.  Enter if
other than A.

It will then prompt for the directory to install the files.  Enter if other
than C:\COP.

After confirming that there is room for the files, INSTALL copies them to
the installation directory.

Pay particular attention to the notes on editing your CONFIG.SYS and
AUTOEXEC.BAT files.  You must properly set up your path and environment
variables.  If you need to redisplay the notes, enter:

   	TYPE ASMREAD.ME


6.0	STANDARD INCLUDE FILES

The assembler package provides standard include files for each member of the
COP8 family of chips.  The files are named as follows:

	COP<chipname>.INC	(Example:  COP888GD.INC)

The first line of each assembly source program should contain the line:

	.INCLD COPxxx.INC

where xxx is the appropriate chip name.

Each include file contains the proper .CHIP directive, and defines symbolic
names for frequently used memory locations.

If you choose not to use an include file, you must include the .CHIP directive
before the beginning of code.


7.0	NOTES AND CAUTIONS

7.1	CAUTIONS FOR ASMCOP

  o  The assembler may run out of memory if macros are nested more than two
     levels deep.

  o  The assembler truncates intermediate results of expression evaluation
     to 16 bits.  As a result, it produces incorrect code for expressions in
     which intermediate values exceed the maximum value that can be represented
     in 16 bits.

     For example, for the following instruction:

	LD A, #(0X101B * 0X64) / 0X679

     The assembler evaluates (0X101B * 0X64) to 0X64A8C and truncates it to
     0X4A8C.  As a result, the value of the complete expression will be 0X0B
     instead of the expected value of 0XF8.

  o  In some cases, the use of primary controls (e.g., #PWIDTH) may apply only
     to the first file.  Subsequent modules may revert to the default condition.


7.2	NOTES FOR ASMCOP

  o  The assembler always interprets the address specified with a JP instruction
     as an absolute address, even though in the instruction opcode it is
     represented as relative.  The conversion from absolute to relative is done
     by the assembler.

     To specify a relative address, use .+<n> or .-<n>

     Example:
   
	.chip  880
	.sect  test,ROM
	start: LD A, #5
	       X A, B
	       IFBIT 2,[B]
	       JP .+2      ; relative jump of +2 (skips over next instr)
			   ; JP +2 would mean the absolute address 2
	       ADD A,#15
	       IFBIT 5,[B]
	       JP start    ; relative jump of -10 (to label start:)
	.end

  o  You must initialize the stack pointer for non-888 type chips.  For example:

	LD SP,#top-of-stack


7.3	NOTES FOR HEXDIFF

  o  HEXDIFF compares LM and/or HEX files for equivalent code, even if the data
     records are in different order.  Command for invocation is:

	HEXDIFF <file1> <file2>


7.4	CAUTIONS FOR HEXLM

  o  No error is given if HEX file contains record type 02, and an erroneous
     LM file is generated.

     Workaround:  Do not convert HEX file with record type 02.


